Pet phase two: reflect the session, and a bubble that says it in words

The pet now wears the current session's state — thinking, running a tool,
waiting for approval, finished, failed — and clicking it opens a small card with
the session title, the state, and how the last turn ended.

State is derived in the host process, not in the page. The pet lives on the
desktop, and the DSH page is gone whenever the main window is closed, which is
exactly when the pet matters. A new host plugin subscribes to `session/event` and
publishes `GET /dsh-desktop-pet/state`; the shell polls it and re-emits over
Tauri's own event channel, because the pet is a shell window on a different
origin and cannot fetch the host itself.

The mapping is where this could have gone wrong quietly. `turn/end` does not mean
the turn succeeded — its `reason.kind` can be `completed`, `aborted`, `blocked`,
`error`, `max-tokens` or `interrupted`. The whale-girl plugin treats every
non-blocked end as "done", which would have the pet celebrating failures.
Measured on a real turn here: idle -> thinking -> error -> idle, with
`reason.kind === 'error'` and `reason.error.code === 'SERVER'`.

Two bugs found along the way, both of which broke more than the pet:

* Login cookies accumulated until the app could not open. The host mints a
  30-day `dsh-auth-<token>` cookie per launch and nothing removes them. Measured:
  69 cookies, about 15.6 KB of Cookie header. Chromium refuses to send headers
  over roughly 2 KB, so the app's own page failed with `431 Request Header Fields
  Too Large` before running a line. Pruned before the content webview's first
  request, and on its own thread — Tauri documents these cookie calls as
  deadlocking from a sync command.

* The bubble window was missing from capabilities/default.json. Permissions are
  per-window, so it rendered perfectly and silently received no `pet-state`
  events at all, which reads as "the bubble is not wired up". Same trap the pet
  hit in phase one.

Also worth recording, and not yet fixed: force-killing the shell skips
`RunEvent::Exit`, orphaning the DSH host it spawned. Twelve were found here, the
oldest from 9/21, each holding session write leases — which makes sending a
message fail with `SessionAlreadyOwnedError`. See section 12.4 of the design doc.
